1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,942
Aarav reads the tiny print
on his phone charger.

2
00:00:03,942 --> 00:00:04,950
Five volts.

3
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:06,012
Two amperes.

4
00:00:06,362 --> 00:00:08,209
Two numbers on every charger.

5
00:00:08,209 --> 00:00:10,111
But what do they really mean?

6
00:00:10,611 --> 00:00:13,513
To answer that, we start
with electric charge.

7
00:00:13,863 --> 00:00:16,854
Charge is a property of tiny particles.

8
00:00:16,854 --> 00:00:19,127
Protons carry positive charge.

9
00:00:19,127 --> 00:00:21,513
Electrons carry negative charge.

10
00:00:21,863 --> 00:00:24,114
Like charges push each other away.

11
00:00:24,114 --> 00:00:26,004
Unlike charges pull together.

12
00:00:26,654 --> 00:00:30,354
Charge is measured in coulombs,
written with a capital C.

13
00:00:30,704 --> 00:00:33,057
And charge comes in fixed packets.

14
00:00:33,057 --> 00:00:36,722
The smallest packet is the
charge of one electron, e.

15
00:00:36,722 --> 00:00:40,918
That is one point six times ten
to the minus nineteen coulombs.

16
00:00:41,268 --> 00:00:44,649
So any charge Q is a
whole number of packets.

17
00:00:44,649 --> 00:00:46,285
Q equals n times e.

18
00:00:46,285 --> 00:00:51,619
One coulomb is about six point two five
times ten to the eighteen electrons.

19
00:00:52,269 --> 00:00:53,318
Now, current.

20
00:00:53,668 --> 00:00:57,312
When charge moves along a wire,
we get an electric current.

21
00:00:57,662 --> 00:01:01,488
Stand at one point and count the
charge that passes every second.

22
00:01:01,488 --> 00:01:03,120
That rate is the current.

23
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:05,939
So I equals Q divided by t.

24
00:01:06,639 --> 00:01:08,799
Current is measured in amperes.

25
00:01:08,799 --> 00:01:12,122
One ampere means one coulomb
passing every second.

26
00:01:12,772 --> 00:01:14,552
Remember from episode one.

27
00:01:14,552 --> 00:01:16,899
Electrons drift from minus to plus.

28
00:01:16,899 --> 00:01:19,754
Conventional current I
points the other way.

29
00:01:20,404 --> 00:01:22,625
But why do the charges move at all?

30
00:01:22,625 --> 00:01:23,826
They need a push.

31
00:01:24,176 --> 00:01:26,895
Think of two water tanks joined by a pipe.

32
00:01:26,895 --> 00:01:30,387
Water flows only when one
tank is higher than the other.

33
00:01:30,737 --> 00:01:33,746
In a circuit, the height
is electric potential.

34
00:01:33,746 --> 00:01:37,872
Charges flow when there is a potential
difference between two points.

35
00:01:38,222 --> 00:01:42,249
Potential difference is the work done
to move one coulomb of charge from

36
00:01:42,249 --> 00:01:43,794
one point to the other.

37
00:01:44,144 --> 00:01:47,089
So V equals W divided by Q.

38
00:01:47,789 --> 00:01:49,518
It is measured in volts.

39
00:01:49,518 --> 00:01:52,876
One volt means one joule
of work for every coulomb.

40
00:01:53,526 --> 00:01:57,534
And voltage is simply the everyday
name for potential difference.

41
00:01:58,183 --> 00:02:00,197
Let's watch both on the breadboard.

42
00:02:00,547 --> 00:02:03,091
One cell, one switch, and a resistor.

43
00:02:03,091 --> 00:02:05,926
A voltmeter reads the
voltage across the cell.

44
00:02:06,276 --> 00:02:10,871
With the switch open, the cell still
has one point five volts across it.

45
00:02:10,871 --> 00:02:12,348
But no current flows.

46
00:02:12,698 --> 00:02:14,037
Close the switch.

47
00:02:14,037 --> 00:02:15,648
Now the charges move.

48
00:02:15,648 --> 00:02:18,713
The meter reads zero
point one five amperes.

49
00:02:19,363 --> 00:02:20,690
Add a second cell.

50
00:02:20,690 --> 00:02:22,800
The push doubles to three volts.

51
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:24,363
Watch the scope.

52
00:02:24,363 --> 00:02:26,336
Both traces step up together.

53
00:02:26,336 --> 00:02:31,100
The electrons speed up, and the current
rises to zero point three amperes.

54
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:32,869
More push, more flow.

55
00:02:32,869 --> 00:02:35,508
And with no push, there is no flow at all.

56
00:02:36,158 --> 00:02:37,743
Voltage is the push.

57
00:02:37,743 --> 00:02:39,328
Current is the flow.

58
00:02:40,128 --> 00:02:42,890
Now, how much energy does the source give?

59
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:46,576
From the volt, the work
W equals V times Q.

60
00:02:47,276 --> 00:02:49,977
And the charge Q equals I times t.

61
00:02:50,677 --> 00:02:52,267
Put them together.

62
00:02:52,267 --> 00:02:54,683
W equals V times I times t.

63
00:02:55,383 --> 00:02:59,958
Volts times amperes times seconds
gives joules, the unit of energy.

64
00:03:00,608 --> 00:03:01,729
Worked example.

65
00:03:01,729 --> 00:03:05,795
Aarav's charger gives five volts
and two amperes for one minute.

66
00:03:06,145 --> 00:03:07,571
First, the charge.

67
00:03:07,571 --> 00:03:11,978
Q equals two times sixty, which is
one hundred and twenty coulombs.

68
00:03:12,328 --> 00:03:13,660
Then the energy.

69
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:18,785
W equals five times one hundred and
twenty, which is six hundred joules.

70
00:03:19,135 --> 00:03:20,506
And the electrons?

71
00:03:20,506 --> 00:03:25,189
One hundred and twenty, divided by
one point six times ten to the minus

72
00:03:25,189 --> 00:03:26,089
nineteen.

73
00:03:25,973 --> 00:03:29,954
That is seven point five times
ten to the twenty electrons.

74
00:03:30,604 --> 00:03:32,069
Now, the exam trap.

75
00:03:32,419 --> 00:03:35,750
Many students say voltage
flows through a resistor.

76
00:03:35,750 --> 00:03:36,796
It does not.

77
00:03:37,146 --> 00:03:39,304
Current flows through a component.

78
00:03:39,304 --> 00:03:42,402
Voltage is always across
it, between two points.

79
00:03:43,052 --> 00:03:44,721
Where is this asked?

80
00:03:44,721 --> 00:03:46,390
In PUC board papers.

81
00:03:46,390 --> 00:03:51,609
Define current, potential difference
and the volt, and solve short problems

82
00:03:51,609 --> 00:03:55,159
with I equals Q by t, and V equals W by Q.

83
00:03:55,809 --> 00:03:56,709
Try this.

84
00:03:56,618 --> 00:04:01,241
A current of zero point five amperes
flows through a lamp for six seconds,

85
00:04:01,241 --> 00:04:04,287
and the source does
thirty six joules of work.

86
00:04:04,287 --> 00:04:07,422
What is the potential
difference across the lamp?

87
00:04:12,822 --> 00:04:13,722
C.

88
00:04:13,317 --> 00:04:14,549
Twelve volts.

89
00:04:14,899 --> 00:04:16,490
First, the charge.

90
00:04:16,490 --> 00:04:18,159
Q equals I times t.

91
00:04:18,159 --> 00:04:21,419
Zero point five times
six is three coulombs.

92
00:04:21,769 --> 00:04:23,800
Then V equals W by Q.

93
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,384
Thirty six divided by
three is twelve volts.

94
00:04:28,034 --> 00:04:29,555
Screenshot this card.

95
00:04:32,755 --> 00:04:35,471
Next, direct current
and alternating current.

96
00:04:35,471 --> 00:04:39,307
Why does a cell give one kind,
and the wall socket the other?
